    "lem-finite-metric-transport-optima-attained": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty set. A **probability vector** on\n$\\mathcal X$ is a family $P=(p_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$ of real numbers such that\n$p_x\\ge 0$ for every $x$ and $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}p_x=1$. Its support is\n$\\operatorname{supp}P:=\\{x\\in\\mathcal X:p_x>0\\}$. Coordinates outside the\nsupport are retained as zeros; they are not deleted from the alphabet.\n\nFor another probability vector $Q=(q_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$, the notation\n$P\\ll Q$ means $\\operatorname{supp}P\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}Q$. All\nfinite sums over $\\mathcal X$ use the displayed alphabet and ordinary finite\nreal arithmetic, extended to $+\\infty$ only where a later definition says so.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-finite-metric-coupling",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a finite nonempty set with this fixed ordering, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_j)_{j=1}^m$ be probability vectors on $X$: their coordinates are nonnegative real numbers and $\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i=\\sum_{j=1}^m q_j=1$. A **coupling** of $p$ and $q$ is a real matrix $\\pi=(\\pi_{ij})_{i,j=1}^m$ satisfying\n$$\n\\pi_{ij}\\ge 0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=p_i\\quad(1\\le i\\le m),\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=q_j\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n$$\nWrite $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. The first index records the source point and the second index records the destination point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-metric-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]) constructed in this\nlibrary ([[def-real-numbers]]) and carrying its order ([[def-real-order]]).\n\nLet $X$ be a set. A **metric** on $X$ is a function\n$d : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that for all $x, y, z \\in X$:\n\n- **(M1) Separation.** $d(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $x = y$.\n- **(M2) Symmetry.** $d(x,y) = d(y,x)$.\n- **(M3) Triangle inequality.** $d(x,z) \\le d(x,y) + d(y,z)$.\n\nA **metric space** is a pair $(X,d)$ consisting of a set $X$ and a metric $d$ on\nit. The elements of $X$ are its **points** and $d(x,y)$ is the **distance** from\n$x$ to $y$. When only one metric is in play we write $X$ for $(X,d)$; when\nseveral are, the metric is always named.\n\n**The values of a metric are real numbers.** The codomain is $\\mathbb{R}$, so\n$d(x,y)$ is an honest element of the complete ordered field and every\ninequality above is an inequality there. No infinite value is permitted;\n[[rem-metric-axiom-conventions]] records why extended metrics are not treated in\nthis library.\n\n**Nonnegativity is deliberately absent from the axiom list.** Many texts add a\nfourth axiom $d(x,y) \\ge 0$. It is redundant: (M1), (M2) and (M3) already force\nit, as [[lem-metric-nonnegativity]] proves. Nothing below assumes it before that\nlemma is available.\n\n**Pseudometric.** A **pseudometric** on $X$ is a function\n$p : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfying (M2), (M3) and the weakening\n\n- **(M1') Reflexivity.** $p(x,x) = 0$ for every $x \\in X$\n\nof (M1). A pseudometric may therefore assign distance $0$ to two distinct\npoints. Every metric is a pseudometric, and a pseudometric is a metric exactly\nwhen $p(x,y) = 0$ forces $x = y$.\n\n**Ultrametric.** An **ultrametric** on $X$ is a metric $d$ that in addition\nsatisfies\n\n- **(M3') Strong triangle inequality.** $d(x,z) \\le \\max\\{d(x,y), d(y,z)\\}$\n\nfor all $x, y, z \\in X$, where the maximum is that of a two-element subset of\n$\\mathbb{R}$, which exists and is one of the two elements\n([[def-max-min]], [[lem-finite-set-has-max]]). An **ultrametric space** is a pair\n$(X,d)$ with $d$ an ultrametric.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-metric-nonnegativity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $p : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfy the reflexivity\naxiom (M1') $p(x,x) = 0$ and the symmetry axiom (M2) $p(x,y) = p(y,x)$ of\n[[def-metric-space]]. Then:\n\n1. If $p$ satisfies the triangle inequality (M3), then $p(x,y) \\ge 0$ for all\n   $x, y \\in X$.\n2. If $p$ satisfies the strong triangle inequality (M3'), then\n   $p(x,y) \\ge 0$ for all $x, y \\in X$.\n\nIn particular every metric, every pseudometric and every ultrametric\n([[def-metric-space]]) takes only nonnegative values. Nonnegativity is therefore\na theorem about the axiom list this library uses, not a fourth axiom, and no\nstatement on this page needs to assume it separately.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-metrics-on-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$. A von Neumann natural is the set of its\npredecessors, $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]), so it can be\nused directly as an index set. Define\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^n := \\{\\, x : x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\},$$\n\nand write $x_k$ for $x(k)$, $k < n$. Two elements of $\\mathbb{R}^n$ are equal\nexactly when they agree at every $k < n$, functions being equal when they have\nthe same values. For $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^n$ put\n\n$$d_1(x,y) := \\sum_{k<n} |x_k - y_k|, \\qquad d_2(x,y) := \\sqrt{\\ \\sum_{k<n} (x_k - y_k)^2\\ }, \\qquad d_\\infty(x,y) := \\max\\{\\, |x_k - y_k| : k < n \\,\\}.$$\n\nAll three are well defined: the finite sums are those of [[def-finite-sum]]; the\nsum of squares is nonnegative ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]], [[lem-of-square-positive]])\nso it has a unique nonnegative square root ([[thm-of-square-roots]]); and\n$\\{|x_k - y_k| : k < n\\}$ is a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$, because\n$n \\ge 1$, so it has a maximum ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).\n\n**Then $d_1$, $d_2$ and $d_\\infty$ are metrics on $\\mathbb{R}^n$**\n([[def-metric-space]]).\n\n**Why $n \\ge 1$.** For $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^0$ has exactly one element,\nthe empty function, and $d_1$ and $d_2$ are the empty sum $0$ and its root; but\n$d_\\infty$ would be the maximum of the empty set, which does not exist. The\nhypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is therefore not decoration, and it is carried by every\nstatement about $d_\\infty$ in this library.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-metric-topology",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space ([[def-metric-space]]).\n\nA subset $U \\subseteq X$ is **open in $(X,d)$** if for every $x \\in U$ there is a\nreal $r > 0$ with $B(x,r) \\subseteq U$ ([[def-metric-ball]]). A subset\n$F \\subseteq X$ is **closed in $(X,d)$** if its complement $X \\setminus F$ is\nopen.\n\nThe collection\n\n$$\\mathcal{T}_d := \\{\\, U \\subseteq X : U \\text{ is open in } (X,d) \\,\\}$$\n\nof all open subsets is the **metric topology** of $d$ on $X$. A subset of $X$\nthat is both open and closed is called **clopen**.\n\n**Two sets are open for trivial reasons.** $\\emptyset$ is open, because the\ndefining condition quantifies over no points; and $X$ is open, because\n$B(x,r) \\subseteq X$ for every $x$ and every $r > 0$. Consequently $X$ and\n$\\emptyset$ are also closed, and both are clopen.\n\n**A neighbourhood of a point $x$** is any set containing an open set that\ncontains $x$; an **open neighbourhood** is an open set containing $x$. In a\nmetric space, a set is a neighbourhood of $x$ exactly when it contains some\nball $B(x,r)$ with $r>0$. Thus $U$ is open exactly when it is a neighbourhood of\neach of its points, and metric balls suffice to test this condition.\n\n**The metric, not the set, determines $\\mathcal{T}_d$.** Two metrics on the same\nset may have different metric topologies, and two different metrics may have the\nsame one; the systematic comparison is [[def-equivalent-metrics]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-heine-borel-rn",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, let $\\mathbb{R}^n$ be the set of functions\n$n \\to \\mathbb{R}$ and let $d_2$ be the Euclidean metric on it\n([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). Then:\n\n1. **Closed boxes are compact.** For reals $a_k \\le b_k$ $(k < n)$ the box\n   $Q = \\{\\, x \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : a_k \\le x_k \\le b_k \\text{ for every } k < n \\,\\}$\n   is a compact subset of $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ ([[def-metric-compactness]]).\n2. **Heine-Borel.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}^n$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}^n, d_2)$ if and only if $K$ is closed in $\\mathbb{R}^n$\n   ([[def-metric-topology]]) and bounded\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]).\n3. **The real line.** A subset $K \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is a compact subset of\n   $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$, the usual metric $d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) = |x-y|$\n   ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]]), if and only if $K$ is closed in\n   $\\mathbb{R}$ and bounded.\n\n**No choice principle is used.** The bisection below halves one coordinate at a\ntime and takes the **left** half whenever the left half still fails to be finitely\ncovered, the right half otherwise: a rule with two outcomes, decided by a\nproperty of the box, not a selection. That is the whole reason the theorem is\navailable in ZF, while the general \"complete and totally bounded implies compact\"\n([[thm-complete-and-totally-bounded-implies-compact]]) is not.\n\nThe hypothesis $n \\ge 1$ is inherited from [[lem-metrics-on-rn]], which defines\n$\\mathbb{R}^n$ and its metrics only there; the last remark below records what\nhappens at $n = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-euclidean-semicontinuous-extreme-value-theorem",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and let $K\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty and compact. Every lower semicontinuous function $f:K\\to\\mathbb R$ is bounded below and attains a minimum. Dually, every upper semicontinuous function $f:K\\to\\mathbb R$ is bounded above and attains a maximum.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Define $d_{\\mathbb{R}} : \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) := |x - y|$ ([[def-abs-value]]). Then:\n\n1. $d_{\\mathbb{R}}$ is a metric on $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-metric-space]]); it is\n   called the **usual metric** of $\\mathbb{R}$.\n2. For $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $r > 0$ the open ball is the bounded open interval\n   ([[def-interval]], [[def-metric-ball]])\n   $$B(x,r) = (x-r,\\ x+r),$$\n   and the closed ball is $\\bar B(x,r) = [x-r,\\ x+r]$.\n3. Consequently $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is open in the metric topology of\n   $d_{\\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-metric-topology]]) exactly when for every $x \\in U$\n   there is $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \\subseteq U$. This topology is called the\n   **usual topology** of $\\mathbb{R}$.\n4. $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$ is not a bounded metric space\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]): no ball contains $\\mathbb{R}$, so\n   $\\operatorname{diam}(\\mathbb{R})$ is not defined.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-metric-continuity",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X, d_X)$ and $(Y, d_Y)$ be metric spaces ([[def-metric-space]]), let\n$f : X \\to Y$ be a function and let $a \\in X$.\n\n$f$ is **continuous at $a$** if for every real $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real\n$\\delta > 0$ such that\n\n$$d_X(x,a) < \\delta \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; d_Y\\big(f(x), f(a)\\big) < \\varepsilon \\qquad \\text{for all } x \\in X .$$\n\n$f$ is **continuous** (globally, or on $X$) if it is continuous at every point of\n$X$.\n\n**The same condition in balls.** Since $d_X(x,a) < \\delta$ says $x \\in B_X(a,\\delta)$\nand $d_Y(f(x),f(a)) < \\varepsilon$ says $f(x) \\in B_Y(f(a),\\varepsilon)$\n([[def-metric-ball]]), continuity at $a$ reads: for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there\nis $\\delta > 0$ with\n\n$$f\\big[B_X(a,\\delta)\\big] \\subseteq B_Y\\big(f(a), \\varepsilon\\big).$$\n\nBoth forms are used below and are the same statement written twice.\n\n**Both metrics matter, and both are named.** Continuity is a property of the\ntriple $(d_X, d_Y, f)$, not of $f$ alone. When several metrics on the same\nunderlying sets are in play, as in [[def-equivalent-metrics]], the metrics are\nalways written out.\n\n**Quantifier order.** The $\\delta$ is allowed to depend on $\\varepsilon$ **and on\nthe point $a$**. Requiring one $\\delta$ to work at every point simultaneously is\na strictly stronger condition, uniform continuity; it is defined on a later page\nof this library, and at this point in the reading order it is written out in full\nwhere needed ([[def-equivalent-metrics]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-semicontinuity-on-euclidean-subsets",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $A\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, let $f:A\\to\\mathbb R$, and let $a\\in A$. Using the Euclidean metric and its balls ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]], [[def-metric-ball]]), $f$ is upper semicontinuous at $a$ when for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ such that $f(x)<f(a)+\\varepsilon$ for every $x\\in A\\cap B_2(a,\\delta)$.\n\nThe function is **lower semicontinuous at $a$** when for every $\\varepsilon>0$ there is $\\delta>0$ such that\n\n$$f(a)-\\varepsilon<f(x)\\qquad(x\\in A\\cap B_2(a,\\delta)).$$\n\nIt is upper or lower semicontinuous on $A$ when the corresponding condition holds at every point of $A$. These are relative notions on $A$; when $A$ is empty, each on-set condition is vacuous. A continuous function ([[def-metric-continuity]]) satisfies both conditions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-finite-metric-wasserstein-one",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set and let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$. Let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ be probability vectors, so their coordinates are nonnegative and each vector sums to $1$. A coupling is an $m\\times m$ matrix $\\pi$ with $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$, row sums $\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}=p_i$, and column sums $\\sum_i\\pi_{ij}=q_j$; write $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. Writing $d_{ij}=d(x_i,x_j)$, define the finite-metric 1-Wasserstein transport cost by\n$$\nW_1(p,q):=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d_{ij}\\pi_{ij}.\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-of-square-positive",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$. For\nevery $a \\in F$ with $a \\neq 0$ we have $a^2 > 0$, where $a^2 = a \\cdot a$. In\nparticular, every nonzero square is positive.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-integer-power",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $a \\in \\mathbb{R}$, where $\\mathbb{R}$ is the ambient ordered field\n([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-field]]).\n\n**Natural exponents.** By the recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) applied to\nthe set $\\mathbb{R}$, the starting element $1$ and the function\n$f(x) = x \\cdot a$, there is a unique function $\\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$,\nwritten $n \\mapsto a^n$, with\n\n$$a^0 = 1, \\qquad a^{n+1} = a^n \\cdot a \\quad (n \\in \\mathbb{N}).$$\n\nThus $a^1 = a$, $a^2 = a \\cdot a$, and so on. Note that this is defined for\n**every** $a$, including $a = 0$.\n\n**Negative exponents.** If $a \\ne 0$ and $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $n \\ge 1$, set\n\n$$a^{-n} := (a^n)^{-1}.$$\n\n**Why that is legitimate.** The right-hand side presupposes that $a^n$ is\ninvertible, that is, that $a^n \\ne 0$. This is a proof obligation and not an\nobservation, and it is discharged by claim 2 of [[lem-power-laws]]: for $a \\ne 0$\nin a field, $a^n \\ne 0$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, proved there by induction on\n$n$ from the fact that a field has no zero divisors ([[lem-of-no-zero-divisors]]).\nThat lemma is a statement *about* the operation introduced here, so it depends on\nthis definition and is recorded in this item's `justified_by` rather than in its\n`deps` (SCHEMA \u00a73). Given $a^n \\ne 0$, the value $(a^n)^{-1}$ is a single\nwell-determined element, because multiplicative inverses in a field are unique\n([[lem-of-inverse-unique]]).\n\n**Integer exponents.** Every integer $m$ ([[def-integers]]) is either\n$n$ or $-n$ for a unique natural $n$, where $n$ in these integer expressions denotes its image under the\nembedding $\\iota : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{Z}$ ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]],\n[[def-int-operations]]). This too is a citation and not a slogan: the order on\n$\\mathbb{Z}$ is total ([[thm-int-ordered-ring]]), so $m \\ge 0$ or $m < 0$; the\nimage of $\\iota$ is exactly the set of nonnegative integers, and each of them is\n$n$ for a unique natural $n$ ([[lem-nat-embeds-int]]); and if $m < 0$ then\n$-m > 0$, by compatibility of the order with addition ([[thm-int-ordered-ring]]),\nso $-m = n$ and $m = -n$, with $n$ unique because $\\iota$ is\ninjective. The two clauses above therefore define $a^m$ for every\n$m \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ whenever $a \\ne 0$, and for every $m \\in \\mathbb{N}$ for\narbitrary $a$. The two exponent domains are disjoint: the natural clause\nhandles $m \\ge 0$, including $m = 0$, while the negative clause requires\n$n \\ge 1$ and therefore handles only $m < 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-of-zero-mult",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In any field $F$ ([[def-field]]), for every $a \\in F$ we have $0 \\cdot a = 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-finite-sum-laws",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $a, b, c : \\mathbb{N} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ be sequences of reals, let\n$\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$, and let $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$, with finite sums and\nfinite products as in [[def-finite-sum]]. Then:\n\n1. **Additivity.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n}(a_k + b_k) = \\sum_{k<n} a_k + \\sum_{k<n} b_k$.\n2. **Scaling.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} \\lambda a_k = \\lambda \\sum_{k<n} a_k$; in particular $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} \\lambda = n\\lambda$, where $n$ denotes the canonical field image $n\\cdot1_{\\mathbb{R}}$ of the natural n ([[lem-of-q-embeds]], [[lem-of-naturals-positive]]).\n3. **Splitting.** If $m \\le n$ then $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} a_k = \\sum_{k<m} a_k + \\sum_{k=m}^{n-1} a_k$, and $\\displaystyle\\prod_{k<n} a_k = \\Big(\\prod_{k<m} a_k\\Big)\\Big(\\prod_{k=m}^{n-1} a_k\\Big)$.\n4. **Monotonicity.** If $a_k \\le b_k$ for all $k < n$ then $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n} a_k \\le \\sum_{k<n} b_k$. In particular, if $a_k \\ge 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\\sum_{k<n} a_k \\ge 0$, every single term satisfies $a_j \\le \\sum_{k<n} a_k$ for $j < n$, and $\\sum_{k<n} a_k = 0$ forces $a_k = 0$ for every $k < n$.\n5. **Telescoping.** $\\displaystyle\\sum_{k<n}\\big(c_{k+1} - c_k\\big) = c_n - c_0$.\n6. **Products.** $\\displaystyle\\prod_{k<n}(a_k b_k) = \\Big(\\prod_{k<n} a_k\\Big)\\Big(\\prod_{k<n} b_k\\Big)$; if $a_k \\ge 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\\prod_{k<n} a_k \\ge 0$, and if $a_k > 0$ for all $k < n$ then $\\prod_{k<n} a_k > 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-of-square-monotone",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field. For $a, b \\ge 0$:\n\n$$a < b \\iff a^2 < b^2, \\qquad a \\le b \\iff a^2 \\le b^2.$$\n\nMore generally, for arbitrary $a, b \\in F$, $a^2 < b^2 \\iff |a| < |b|$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "thm-of-square-roots",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]). Then every\n$a \\in F$ with $a \\ge 0$ has a **unique** $s \\in F$ with $s \\ge 0$ and $s^2 = a$;\nwe write $s = \\sqrt{a}$. Consequently the positive elements of $F$ are exactly the\nnonzero squares: $x > 0$ if and only if $x = y^2$ for some $y \\neq 0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-abs-value",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). The **absolute value** of\n$x \\in F$ is\n\n$$|x| \\;:=\\; \\begin{cases} x & \\text{if } x \\ge 0, \\\\ -x & \\text{if } x < 0. \\end{cases}$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-of-abs-value",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and let $x, y \\in F$, with\n$|x|$ the absolute value ([[def-abs-value]]). Then\n\n$$|x| \\ge 0; \\quad |x| = 0 \\iff x = 0; \\quad -|x| \\le x \\le |x|; \\quad |-x| = |x|; \\quad |xy| = |x|\\,|y|;$$\n\nand, for every $c > 0$, one has $|x| < c \\iff -c < x < c$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-of-triangle-inequality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and let $x, y \\in F$. Then\n\n$$|x + y| \\le |x| + |y|.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "lem-of-sign-rules",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) with positive cone $P$, and\nlet $a, b, c \\in F$.\n\n1. If $a > 0$ and $b > 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n2. If $a > 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab < 0$.\n3. If $a < 0$ and $b < 0$ then $ab > 0$.\n4. If $c > 0$ then $a < b \\iff ac < bc$.\n5. If $c < 0$ then $a < b \\iff bc < ac$ (multiplication by a negative reverses order).",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F12",
          "source": "thm-induction-principle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{N}$. If $0 \\in S$ and $\\sigma(n) \\in S$ whenever $n \\in S$, then $S = \\mathbb{N}$. Equivalently (property form): if a property $P$ satisfies $P(0)$ and ($P(n) \\Rightarrow P(\\sigma(n))$) for all $n$, then $P(n)$ holds for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. This is the induction principle, the basis of proof by induction.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Define $\\pi_{ij}=p_iq_j$. These entries are nonnegative by [F1], and their row sums are $p_i\\sum_jq_j=p_i$ while their column sums are $q_j\\sum_ip_i=q_j$. Thus $\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)$ and the coupling set is nonempty.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Flatten a matrix into $\\mathbb R^{m^2}$ in row-major order, assigning entry $\\pi_{ij}$ to library coordinate $k=(i-1)m+(j-1)<m^2$; likewise potential coordinate $f_i$ corresponds to library coordinate $i-1<m$. In any dimension $n\\ge1$, for $u\\in\\mathbb R^n$ and $k<n$, [F11] gives $u_k^2\\le\\sum_{j<n}u_j^2$; by [F5,F11], $|u_k|^2=u_k^2\\le d_2(u,0)^2$, and both $|u_k|$ and $d_2(u,0)$ are nonnegative, so [F11] gives $|u_k|\\le d_2(u,0)$. By induction on $n$, every finite list satisfies $|\\sum_{k<n}a_k|\\le\\sum_{k<n}|a_k|$: the empty case is equality and the successor step follows from $|a+b|\\le|a|+|b|$ in [F11]. Thus for every finite linear form $L(z)=\\sum_{k<n}c_kz_k$, this bound, $|ab|=|a||b|$, the coordinate estimate, multiplication by nonnegative coefficients, finite-sum monotonicity and scaling in [F11] give $|L(z)-L(w)|\\le(\\sum_{k<n}|c_k|)d_2(z,w)$. The same estimate holds for every affine form $H(z)=L(z)+b$, since the constant cancels in $H(z)-H(w)$. The set $\\Gamma(p,q)$ is defined by finitely many affine equalities and weak inequalities. Each row or column equality, after moving its marginal to one side, has $m$ coefficients equal to $1$; each entrywise nonnegativity constraint has one coefficient equal to $1$, so the coefficient sum $C=\\sum|c_k|$ is positive for every constraint. If an affine equality $H(z)=0$ fails at $z_0$, then for $d_2(z,z_0)<|H(z_0)|/(2C)$ the estimate gives $|H(z)-H(z_0)|<|H(z_0)|/2$, so $H(z)\\ne0$. If an affine inequality $H(z)\\ge0$ fails at $z_0$, then for $d_2(z,z_0)<-H(z_0)/(2C)$ the estimate gives $H(z)<H(z_0)/2<0$. Thus every point outside $\\Gamma(p,q)$ has a ball in its complement, so [F5,F6] make $\\Gamma(p,q)$ closed. Also $0\\le\\pi_{ij}\\le p_i\\le1$ by [F1,F2], so the set is bounded. Since $m^2\\ge1$, [F6] makes $\\Gamma(p,q)$ compact.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F11",
            "F12",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For a matrix difference $z-w$, the objective changes by at most $C d_2(z,w)$, where $C=\\sum_{i,j}d(x_i,x_j)\\ge0$ is finite; the coordinate bound is the same as in step 1.2. For any $\\varepsilon>0$, taking $\\delta=\\varepsilon/(C+1)$ shows that inputs within $\\delta$ have output difference less than $\\varepsilon$, so the objective is continuous by [F8] and lower semicontinuous by [F9]. It is real-valued because all distances are finite reals; by [F4] and [F1] its values are nonnegative. The nonempty compact set from steps 1.1\u20131.2 and [F7] therefore give a finite attained minimum, which is $W_1(p,q)$ by [F10].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F9",
            "F10",
            "F11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "The set $\\mathcal L_1(X,d)$ contains the zero vector. It is closed because its constraints are finitely many linear equalities and weak inequalities: $f_1=0$ has one nonzero coefficient, and writing each $|f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)$ as $d(x_i,x_j)-(f_i-f_j)\\ge0$ and $d(x_i,x_j)+(f_i-f_j)\\ge0$ gives two nonzero coefficients in each inequality when $i\\ne j$; when $i=j$ the constraint is the identity $0\\le0$. Thus every violated constraint has positive coefficient sum and the strict-violation ball argument in step 1.2 applies using [F5,F6,F11]. Since $f_1=0$ and $|f_i-f_1|\\le d(x_i,x_1)$, every $f\\in\\mathcal L_1(X,d)$ satisfies $|f_i|\\le d(x_i,x_1)\\le 1+\\sum_{k=1}^m d(x_k,x_1)$ by [F3,F4]; thus the set is bounded. Since $m\\ge1$, [F6] makes it compact.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F11",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The objective $f\\mapsto\\sum_i(p_i-q_i)f_i$ changes by at most $C'd_2(f,g)$, where $C'=\\sum_i|p_i-q_i|\\ge0$; taking $\\delta=\\varepsilon/(C'+1)$ for each $\\varepsilon>0$ proves continuity by [F8] and upper semicontinuity by [F9]. The nonempty compact set from step 2.2 and [F7] give an attained maximum.",
