      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-61",
        "notes": "Read and independently checked all five steps and all twenty-three direct supplier statements/definitions, including the complete Euclidean semicontinuous extreme-value proof. The explicit product matrix has both marginals even at zero coordinates. Row-major coordinates cover R^(m^2); coordinate and finite-sum bounds prove affine violation balls and closedness. Entries in [0,1] give Euclidean boundedness, so Heine\u2013Borel applies with m^2>=1. The cost objective is finite and continuous using delta=epsilon/(C+1), also valid when C=0; existence is established before identifying the minimum as W_1. The zero potential is feasible, diagonal constraints are identities, nontrivial constraints have positive coefficient sums, and f_1=0 bounds every coordinate by d(x_i,x_1). Heine\u2013Borel and upper semicontinuous extreme values give the normalized dual maximum, including p=q, singleton X and zero coordinate cases. No uniqueness is claimed and no sequential choice is used. Source context checked: Peyre\u2013Cuturi \u00a72.3 (2.10)\u2013(2.11), \u00a73.1 and Santambrogio \u00a7\u00a71.1\u20131.2 PDF pp.3\u20135; local finite-dimensional proof does not import an unproved inf-sup exchange. Reader/refuter contain no defect; no repair needed."
      }
    },
    "thm-finite-metric-transport-duality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-metric-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Throughout, $\\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field\n([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]) constructed in this\nlibrary ([[def-real-numbers]]) and carrying its order ([[def-real-order]]).\n\nLet $X$ be a set. A **metric** on $X$ is a function\n$d : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ such that for all $x, y, z \\in X$:\n\n- **(M1) Separation.** $d(x,y) = 0$ if and only if $x = y$.\n- **(M2) Symmetry.** $d(x,y) = d(y,x)$.\n- **(M3) Triangle inequality.** $d(x,z) \\le d(x,y) + d(y,z)$.\n\nA **metric space** is a pair $(X,d)$ consisting of a set $X$ and a metric $d$ on\nit. The elements of $X$ are its **points** and $d(x,y)$ is the **distance** from\n$x$ to $y$. When only one metric is in play we write $X$ for $(X,d)$; when\nseveral are, the metric is always named.\n\n**The values of a metric are real numbers.** The codomain is $\\mathbb{R}$, so\n$d(x,y)$ is an honest element of the complete ordered field and every\ninequality above is an inequality there. No infinite value is permitted;\n[[rem-metric-axiom-conventions]] records why extended metrics are not treated in\nthis library.\n\n**Nonnegativity is deliberately absent from the axiom list.** Many texts add a\nfourth axiom $d(x,y) \\ge 0$. It is redundant: (M1), (M2) and (M3) already force\nit, as [[lem-metric-nonnegativity]] proves. Nothing below assumes it before that\nlemma is available.\n\n**Pseudometric.** A **pseudometric** on $X$ is a function\n$p : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfying (M2), (M3) and the weakening\n\n- **(M1') Reflexivity.** $p(x,x) = 0$ for every $x \\in X$\n\nof (M1). A pseudometric may therefore assign distance $0$ to two distinct\npoints. Every metric is a pseudometric, and a pseudometric is a metric exactly\nwhen $p(x,y) = 0$ forces $x = y$.\n\n**Ultrametric.** An **ultrametric** on $X$ is a metric $d$ that in addition\nsatisfies\n\n- **(M3') Strong triangle inequality.** $d(x,z) \\le \\max\\{d(x,y), d(y,z)\\}$\n\nfor all $x, y, z \\in X$, where the maximum is that of a two-element subset of\n$\\mathbb{R}$, which exists and is one of the two elements\n([[def-max-min]], [[lem-finite-set-has-max]]). An **ultrametric space** is a pair\n$(X,d)$ with $d$ an ultrametric.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-metric-nonnegativity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a set and let $p : X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfy the reflexivity\naxiom (M1') $p(x,x) = 0$ and the symmetry axiom (M2) $p(x,y) = p(y,x)$ of\n[[def-metric-space]]. Then:\n\n1. If $p$ satisfies the triangle inequality (M3), then $p(x,y) \\ge 0$ for all\n   $x, y \\in X$.\n2. If $p$ satisfies the strong triangle inequality (M3'), then\n   $p(x,y) \\ge 0$ for all $x, y \\in X$.\n\nIn particular every metric, every pseudometric and every ultrametric\n([[def-metric-space]]) takes only nonnegative values. Nonnegativity is therefore\na theorem about the axiom list this library uses, not a fourth axiom, and no\nstatement on this page needs to assume it separately.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty set. A **probability vector** on\n$\\mathcal X$ is a family $P=(p_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$ of real numbers such that\n$p_x\\ge 0$ for every $x$ and $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}p_x=1$. Its support is\n$\\operatorname{supp}P:=\\{x\\in\\mathcal X:p_x>0\\}$. Coordinates outside the\nsupport are retained as zeros; they are not deleted from the alphabet.\n\nFor another probability vector $Q=(q_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$, the notation\n$P\\ll Q$ means $\\operatorname{supp}P\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}Q$. All\nfinite sums over $\\mathcal X$ use the displayed alphabet and ordinary finite\nreal arithmetic, extended to $+\\infty$ only where a later definition says so.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-finite-metric-coupling",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a finite nonempty set with this fixed ordering, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_j)_{j=1}^m$ be probability vectors on $X$: their coordinates are nonnegative real numbers and $\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i=\\sum_{j=1}^m q_j=1$. A **coupling** of $p$ and $q$ is a real matrix $\\pi=(\\pi_{ij})_{i,j=1}^m$ satisfying\n$$\n\\pi_{ij}\\ge 0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=p_i\\quad(1\\le i\\le m),\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=q_j\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n$$\nWrite $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. The first index records the source point and the second index records the destination point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-finite-metric-wasserstein-one",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set and let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$. Let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ be probability vectors, so their coordinates are nonnegative and each vector sums to $1$. A coupling is an $m\\times m$ matrix $\\pi$ with $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$, row sums $\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}=p_i$, and column sums $\\sum_i\\pi_{ij}=q_j$; write $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. Writing $d_{ij}=d(x_i,x_j)$, define the finite-metric 1-Wasserstein transport cost by\n$$\nW_1(p,q):=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d_{ij}\\pi_{ij}.\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-finite-metric-transport-optima-attained",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite metric space, and let $p,q$ be probability vectors on $X$. The coupling set $\\Gamma(p,q)$ is nonempty and compact in $\\mathbb R^{m^2}$, so the finite-metric transport cost $W_1(p,q)$ attains its minimum. The normalized 1-Lipschitz potential set\n$$\n\\mathcal L_1(X,d)=\\{f\\in\\mathbb R^m:f_1=0,\\ |f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)\\text{ for all }i,j\\}\n$$\nis also nonempty and compact, so the linear functional $f\\mapsto\\sum_{i=1}^m(p_i-q_i)f_i$ attains a maximum on it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "6.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-finitely-generated-cone-is-closed",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r,N\\ge1$ be integers and let $g_1,\\ldots,g_N\\in\\mathbb R^r$. Define\n$$\nC=\\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^N\\lambda_i g_i:\\lambda_i\\ge0\\text{ for every }i\\right\\}.\n$$\nThen $C$ is closed in the Euclidean topology, and for all $x,y\\in C$ and all real $\\alpha,\\beta\\ge0$, one has $\\alpha x+\\beta y\\in C$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-strict-separation-of-a-point-from-a-closed-convex-set",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$, let $C\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be nonempty, closed, and convex, and let $x\\notin C$. Then there are $a\\ne0$ and $b\\in\\mathbb R$ such that $\\langle a,z\\rangle\\le b<\\langle a,x\\rangle$ for every $z\\in C$. Thus a hyperplane strictly separates $x$ from $C$ ([[def-supporting-hyperplane-to-a-convex-set]]).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "lem-finite-set-has-max",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$ and all $a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n \\in \\mathbb{R}$, the\nset $\\{a_0, a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ has a maximum and a minimum ([[def-max-min]]).\n\nWhat is proved below is exactly the displayed statement, by induction on $n$.\n\nThe usual reading, that *every nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ has a\nmaximum and a minimum*, follows once one identifies the nonempty finite subsets\nof $\\mathbb{R}$ with the sets listable as $\\{a_0, \\dots, a_n\\}$. That\nidentification is recorded as a stipulation in the Given below, because this page\nhas no definition of finiteness to prove it against. **It is discharged, not\nmerely assumed**: [[lem-finite-subsets-listable]] proves that the two\ndescriptions of a nonempty finite subset of $\\mathbb{R}$ agree. That lemma is\nrecorded in `justified_by` rather than in `deps`, since it is about the sets this\nlemma quantifies over and therefore depends on this one. This is what licenses\nthe notation\n$\\max\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ and $\\min\\{a_1, \\dots, a_n\\}$ for finite sets of\n**real** numbers from this page onwards.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F9",
          "source": "def-max-min",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ and $m \\in \\mathbb{R}$.\n\n- $m$ is a **maximum** (or *greatest element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $s \\le m$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n- $m$ is a **minimum** (or *least element*) of $S$ if $m \\in S$ and $m \\le s$\n  for every $s \\in S$.\n\nA set has at most one maximum: if $m_1$ and $m_2$ are both maxima then\n$m_1 \\in S$ gives $m_1 \\le m_2$ and $m_2 \\in S$ gives $m_2 \\le m_1$, so\n$m_1 = m_2$ by antisymmetry of the order, which is immediate from the trichotomy\naxiom of an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).\nThe same argument applies to minima, so we may write $\\max S$ and $\\min S$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F10",
          "source": "def-euclidean-inner-product",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$. A natural number is a von Neumann natural, that is a set,\nand $n = \\{0, 1, \\dots, n-1\\}$ ([[def-natural-numbers]],\n[[lem-nat-order-is-membership]]), so\n\n$$\\mathbb{R}^{n} \\;=\\; \\{\\, x \\;:\\; x \\text{ is a function } n \\to \\mathbb{R} \\,\\}$$\n\nis the function space of [[def-function-space]] at $F = \\mathbb{R}$ and $X = n$,\na vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ under the pointwise operations\n([[def-vector-space]]). We write $x_k := x(k)$ for $k < n$, and two elements of\n$\\mathbb{R}^{n}$ are equal exactly when they agree at every $k < n$. This is the\nsame set that [[lem-metrics-on-rn]] calls $\\mathbb{R}^{n}$.\n\nThe **Euclidean inner product** of $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ is the real number\n\n$$\\langle x, y\\rangle \\;:=\\; \\sum_{k<n} x_k\\,y_k ,$$\n\nthe finite sum of [[def-finite-sum]] applied to the list $k \\mapsto x_k y_k$\n(extended by $0$ beyond $n$, as every finite list in this library is). The\n**Euclidean norm** of $x$ is\n\n$$\\lVert x\\rVert_2 \\;:=\\; \\sqrt{\\langle x, x\\rangle},$$\n\nwhich is defined because $\\langle x,x\\rangle = \\sum_{k<n} x_k^{2} \\ge 0$ (a sum\nof nonnegative terms, [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4 and\n[[lem-of-square-positive]], the case $x_k = 0$ giving $x_k^2 = 0$ by\n[[def-integer-power]]) and every nonnegative real has a unique nonnegative square\nroot ([[thm-of-square-roots]]).\n\n### Both are defined for every $n$, including $n = 0$\n\nAt $n = 0$ the set $\\mathbb{R}^{0}$ has exactly one element, the empty function,\nand it is the zero vector space ([[lem-standard-basis-of-f-n]] clause 5); the sum\nabove is the empty sum, so $\\langle x,y\\rangle = 0$ and $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = 0$.\n**This is the first place on this page where the two index regimes diverge, and\nthe divergence is deliberate.** The published metrics $d_1$, $d_2$, $d_\\infty$ of\n[[lem-metrics-on-rn]] are defined only for $n \\ge 1$, because $d_\\infty$ would\notherwise be a maximum over the empty index set; the algebra above needs no such\nrestriction. The boundary in this page runs between the algebra and the metric,\nnot where a reader would guess, and [[rem-rn-conventions-and-scope]] lists\nexactly which items inherit $n \\ge 1$.\n\n### The algebra of the inner product\n\nFor all $x, y, z \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$ and $\\lambda \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n\n1. **Symmetry.** $\\langle x,y\\rangle = \\langle y,x\\rangle$, since $x_ky_k = y_kx_k$\n   termwise.\n2. **Additivity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle x + y, z\\rangle = \\langle x,z\\rangle + \\langle y,z\\rangle$: the list\n   $k \\mapsto (x_k+y_k)z_k$ is the termwise sum of $k \\mapsto x_kz_k$ and\n   $k \\mapsto y_kz_k$, so [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 1 applies.\n3. **Homogeneity in the first argument.**\n   $\\langle \\lambda x, y\\rangle = \\lambda\\langle x,y\\rangle$, by\n   [[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 2.\n4. **Bilinearity.** Clauses 2 and 3 together with symmetry give the same two laws\n   in the second argument.\n5. **Positive definiteness.** $\\langle x,x\\rangle \\ge 0$, and\n   $\\langle x,x\\rangle = 0$ if and only if $x = 0$. Indeed a vanishing sum of\n   nonnegative terms has every term $0$ ([[lem-finite-sum-laws]] clause 4), so\n   $x_k^{2} = 0$ for every $k < n$, and a nonzero real has a positive square\n   ([[lem-of-square-positive]]), whence $x_k = 0$ for every $k < n$ and $x = 0$.\n6. **Agreement with the published Euclidean metric.** For $n \\ge 1$ and\n   $x, y \\in \\mathbb{R}^{n}$,\n   $\\lVert x - y\\rVert_2 = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k<n}(x_k-y_k)^{2}} = d_2(x,y)$, the two\n   sides being the same expression ([[lem-metrics-on-rn]]). In particular\n   $\\lVert x\\rVert_2 = d_2(x, 0)$.\n\nThat $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ is a norm in the sense of [[def-norm-and-normed-space]]\nis proved in [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], where the triangle\ninequality is obtained from the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality; it is not assumed\nhere.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F11",
          "source": "def-convex-subset-of-euclidean-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A subset $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ is **convex** when, for all $x,y\\in U$ and $t\\in[0,1]$ ([[def-interval]]), the point $(1-t)x+ty$ lies in $U$. Thus the full line segment from $x$ to $y$ remains in $U$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For any $\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)$ and any $f\\in\\mathbb R^m$ with $f_1=0$ and $|f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)$, the marginal equations give $\\sum_i(p_i-q_i)f_i=\\sum_{i,j}\\pi_{ij}(f_i-f_j)\\le\\sum_{i,j}\\pi_{ij}d(x_i,x_j)$. The inequality uses $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$ and $f_i-f_j\\le|f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)$. Thus every feasible dual value is at most every feasible primal cost.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Write $d_{ij}=d(x_i,x_j)$, and for $i=1,\\ldots,m$ let $e_i\\in\\mathbb R^m$ have value $1$ at library coordinate $k=i-1$ and $0$ at every other coordinate. For alphabet-indexed tuples $u=(u_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $v=(v_j)_{j=1}^m$, identify $(u,v,t)$ with the vector in $\\mathbb R^{2m+1}$ whose coordinate $k<m$ is $u_{k+1}$, whose coordinate $m+k$ is $v_{k+1}$, and whose coordinate $2m$ is $t$. Let $K$ be the nonnegative span of $(e_i,e_j,d_{ij})$ for all $i,j$, together with $(0,0,1)$. By [F7], $K$ is closed and closed under nonnegative linear combinations. For $x,y\\in K$ and $s\\in[0,1]$, [F7] gives $sx+(1-s)y\\in K$, so [F11] makes $K$ convex; the zero representation gives $0\\in K$. Put $c_*=W_1(p,q)$, which is finite and attained by [F6]. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, the vector $y_\\varepsilon=(p,q,c_*-\\varepsilon)$ under this identification is outside $K$: if it had a representation with coefficients $\\lambda_{ij}\\ge0$ and $t\\ge0$, its first two coordinate blocks would make $\\lambda\\in\\Gamma(p,q)$ and its last coordinate would give $c_*-\\varepsilon=\\sum_{i,j}d_{ij}\\lambda_{ij}+t\\ge c_*$, contradicting minimality.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "F11"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F8] to $K$ and $y_\\varepsilon$. It gives a vector $h$ and threshold $\\beta$ with $\\langle h,z\\rangle\\le\\beta<\\langle h,y_\\varepsilon\\rangle$ for all $z\\in K$. Since $0\\in K$, $\\beta\\ge0$. By [F7], $s z\\in K$ for every $z\\in K$ and $s\\ge0$. If $c:=\\langle h,z\\rangle>0$ for some $z\\in K$, take $s=(\\beta+1)/c>0$; then $sz\\in K$ but $\\langle h,sz\\rangle=\\beta+1>\\beta$, a contradiction. Hence $\\langle h,z\\rangle\\le0$ for every $z\\in K$, while $\\langle h,y_\\varepsilon\\rangle>0$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F7",
            "F8",
            "F10",
            "step 1.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Decompose $h=(a,b,g)$ with $a,b\\in\\mathbb R^m$ and $g\\in\\mathbb R$. By [F10], pairing with $(0,0,1)\\in K$ gives $g\\le0$. If $g=0$, pairing with every $(e_i,e_j,d_{ij})$ gives $a_i+b_j\\le0$. Multiplying by $p_iq_j\\ge0$ and summing yields $\\sum_i p_i a_i+\\sum_jq_jb_j=\\sum_{i,j}p_iq_j(a_i+b_j)\\le0$. When $g=0$ this left-hand side is $\\langle h,y_\\varepsilon\\rangle$, contrary to step 2.1. Therefore $g<0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "F3",
            "F10",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Define $u_i=a_i/(-g)$ and $v_j=b_j/(-g)$. The generator inequalities and $g<0$ give $u_i+v_j\\le d_{ij}$ for all $i,j$. Dividing the strict point inequality by $-g>0$ gives $\\sum_i p_i u_i+\\sum_jq_jv_j>c_*-\\varepsilon$.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "Set $f_i=\\min_{1\\le j\\le m}(d_{ij}-v_j)$, whose minimum is attained by [F9]. From $u_i+v_j\\le d_{ij}$ we have $u_i\\le f_i$. For any $i,k,j$, the triangle inequality gives $d_{ij}-v_j\\le d_{ik}+d_{kj}-v_j$; taking finite minima yields $f_i\\le d_{ik}+f_k$, and symmetry gives $|f_i-f_k|\\le d_{ik}$. Also $f_j\\le d_{jj}-v_j=-v_j$, so $-f_j\\ge v_j$. Since $p,q$ are nonnegative, $\\sum_i(p_i-q_i)f_i\\ge\\sum_i p_i u_i+\\sum_jq_jv_j>c_*-\\varepsilon$. Replacing $f_i$ by $f_i-f_1$ preserves all Lipschitz inequalities and the objective because $\\sum_i p_i=\\sum_iq_i=1$; the resulting vector is normalized with first coordinate zero.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 4.1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F9",
            "algebra"
          ]
