      "risk_review": {
        "status": "complete",
        "reviewer": "alpha-batch-61",
        "notes": "Independently read all eight proof steps, every declared supplier, and the nearest-point and variational-inequality proofs behind strict separation. Weak duality has the source-minus-destination sign. K in R^(2m+1) has m^2+1 generators, is closed, nonempty and convex; its marginal blocks exclude (p,q,c_*-epsilon) by primal minimality. Cone scaling with s=(beta+1)/c proves the correct separator sign; pairing the upward cost generator gives g<=0 and weighting all pair generators by p_i q_j rules out g=0 without any positivity assumption on individual masses. Division by -g preserves the inequalities. The finite c-transform dominates u, its negative dominates v, and metric triangle/symmetry give both Lipschitz directions; subtracting f_1 preserves objective because total masses agree. Epsilon contradiction and attained extrema yield equality. The nonnegative finite gap sum vanishes termwise for every optimal pair, with no converse or uniqueness assumed. Singleton X, p=q and zero coordinates obey the same argument; no AC or external strong duality used. Peyre\u2013Cuturi \u00a73.1 Remark 3.2, \u00a73.3 Proposition 3.2 and \u00a76.1 Proposition 6.1 checked; local argument supplies its own equality and signs. Santambrogio \u00a7\u00a71.1\u20131.3 has Euclidean scope and a formal inf-sup step, not imported. Reader additions at 2.1 and 7.1 merely expose elementary scaling and zero-sum inferences already sound in the authenticated preimage; audit enrichment, no confirmed defect."
