    "ex-two-point-finite-metric-transport-duality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-metric-coupling",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a finite nonempty set with this fixed ordering, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_j)_{j=1}^m$ be probability vectors on $X$: their coordinates are nonnegative real numbers and $\\sum_{i=1}^m p_i=\\sum_{j=1}^m q_j=1$. A **coupling** of $p$ and $q$ is a real matrix $\\pi=(\\pi_{ij})_{i,j=1}^m$ satisfying\n$$\n\\pi_{ij}\\ge 0,\\qquad \\sum_{j=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=p_i\\quad(1\\le i\\le m),\\qquad \\sum_{i=1}^m\\pi_{ij}=q_j\\quad(1\\le j\\le m).\n$$\nWrite $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. The first index records the source point and the second index records the destination point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-finite-metric-wasserstein-one",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set and let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$. Let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$ and $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ be probability vectors, so their coordinates are nonnegative and each vector sums to $1$. A coupling is an $m\\times m$ matrix $\\pi$ with $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$, row sums $\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}=p_i$, and column sums $\\sum_i\\pi_{ij}=q_j$; write $\\Gamma(p,q)$ for the set of all such matrices. Writing $d_{ij}=d(x_i,x_j)$, define the finite-metric 1-Wasserstein transport cost by\n$$\nW_1(p,q):=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d_{ij}\\pi_{ij}.\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-finite-metric-transport-duality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set, let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$, $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ have nonnegative coordinates with each vector summing to $1$. Let $\\Gamma(p,q)$ consist of matrices $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$ with row sums $p_i$ and column sums $q_j$, and let\n$$\nW_1(p,q)=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d(x_i,x_j)\\pi_{ij}.\n$$\nThen\n$$\nW_1(p,q)=\\max_{\\substack{f\\in\\mathbb R^m,\\ f_1=0\\\\ |f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)\\ \\text{for all }i,j}}\\sum_{i=1}^m(p_i-q_i)f_i.\n$$\nBoth extrema are attained. Moreover, for every minimizing coupling $\\pi$ and every maximizing vector $f$, $\\pi_{ij}>0$ implies $f_i-f_j=d(x_i,x_j)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "If $a\\ge b$, all entries of the displayed matrix are nonnegative, its row sums are $b+(a-b)=a$ and $1-a$, and its column sums are $b$ and $(a-b)+(1-a)=1-b$. Its cost is $\\delta(a-b)$. If $b>a$, the second displayed matrix has nonnegative entries, row sums $a$ and $(b-a)+(1-b)=1-a$, column sums $a+(b-a)=b$ and $1-b$, and cost $\\delta(b-a)$. Thus in either case it is a feasible coupling of cost $\\delta|a-b|$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "If $a\\ge b$, the vector $(0,-\\delta)$ is normalized and satisfies the Lipschitz constraint with equality; its objective is $(b-a)(-\\delta)=\\delta(a-b)$. If $b>a$, the vector $(0,\\delta)$ is normalized and feasible, and its objective is $(b-a)\\delta=\\delta(b-a)$. Thus the displayed potential has value $\\delta|a-b|$ in either case.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
