    "ex-finite-chain-transport-duality": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Define $d_{\\mathbb{R}} : \\mathbb{R} \\times \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ by\n$d_{\\mathbb{R}}(x,y) := |x - y|$ ([[def-abs-value]]). Then:\n\n1. $d_{\\mathbb{R}}$ is a metric on $\\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-metric-space]]); it is\n   called the **usual metric** of $\\mathbb{R}$.\n2. For $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ and $r > 0$ the open ball is the bounded open interval\n   ([[def-interval]], [[def-metric-ball]])\n   $$B(x,r) = (x-r,\\ x+r),$$\n   and the closed ball is $\\bar B(x,r) = [x-r,\\ x+r]$.\n3. Consequently $U \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is open in the metric topology of\n   $d_{\\mathbb{R}}$ ([[def-metric-topology]]) exactly when for every $x \\in U$\n   there is $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \\subseteq U$. This topology is called the\n   **usual topology** of $\\mathbb{R}$.\n4. $(\\mathbb{R}, d_{\\mathbb{R}})$ is not a bounded metric space\n   ([[def-metric-bounded-diameter]]): no ball contains $\\mathbb{R}$, so\n   $\\operatorname{diam}(\\mathbb{R})$ is not defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-finite-metric-transport-duality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=\\{x_1,\\ldots,x_m\\}$ be a nonempty finite set, let $d:X\\times X\\to\\mathbb R$ satisfy $d(x,y)=0$ if and only if $x=y$, $d(x,y)=d(y,x)$, and $d(x,z)\\le d(x,y)+d(y,z)$, and let $p=(p_i)_{i=1}^m$, $q=(q_i)_{i=1}^m$ have nonnegative coordinates with each vector summing to $1$. Let $\\Gamma(p,q)$ consist of matrices $\\pi_{ij}\\ge0$ with row sums $p_i$ and column sums $q_j$, and let\n$$\nW_1(p,q)=\\min_{\\pi\\in\\Gamma(p,q)}\\sum_{i=1}^m\\sum_{j=1}^m d(x_i,x_j)\\pi_{ij}.\n$$\nThen\n$$\nW_1(p,q)=\\max_{\\substack{f\\in\\mathbb R^m,\\ f_1=0\\\\ |f_i-f_j|\\le d(x_i,x_j)\\ \\text{for all }i,j}}\\sum_{i=1}^m(p_i-q_i)f_i.\n$$\nBoth extrema are attained. Moreover, for every minimizing coupling $\\pi$ and every maximizing vector $f$, $\\pi_{ij}>0$ implies $f_i-f_j=d(x_i,x_j)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-finite-probability-vector",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite nonempty set. A **probability vector** on\n$\\mathcal X$ is a family $P=(p_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$ of real numbers such that\n$p_x\\ge 0$ for every $x$ and $\\sum_{x\\in\\mathcal X}p_x=1$. Its support is\n$\\operatorname{supp}P:=\\{x\\in\\mathcal X:p_x>0\\}$. Coordinates outside the\nsupport are retained as zeros; they are not deleted from the alphabet.\n\nFor another probability vector $Q=(q_x)_{x\\in\\mathcal X}$, the notation\n$P\\ll Q$ means $\\operatorname{supp}P\\subseteq\\operatorname{supp}Q$. All\nfinite sums over $\\mathcal X$ use the displayed alphabet and ordinary finite\nreal arithmetic, extended to $+\\infty$ only where a later definition says so.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $r_i=p_i-q_i$ for $0\\le i\\le n$, set $R_{-1}=0$ and $R_n=0$, and use $\\sum_i r_i=0$ from [F3]. Then $r_i=R_i-R_{i-1}$ for every $i$, and expanding the finite sums gives $\\sum_{i=0}^n r_i f_i=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}R_k(f_k-f_{k+1})$. For any feasible 1-Lipschitz vector, $|f_k-f_{k+1}|\\le1$, so each term is at most $|R_k|$ and the dual objective is at most $\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}|R_k|$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The recursively defined potential has $|f_{k+1}-f_k|=|\\operatorname{sgn}(R_k)|\\le1$ on each adjacent pair. For $i<j$, the triangle inequality on real absolute values gives $|f_i-f_j|\\le\\sum_{k=i}^{j-1}|f_{k+1}-f_k|\\le j-i=d(x_i,x_j)$; also $f_0=0$. Thus it is feasible. Its objective, by step 1.1, is $\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}R_k\\operatorname{sgn}(R_k)=\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}|R_k|$, so it attains the dual maximum.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
